<description>
Recover the rectangular green mask from the unique visible symmetric copies.

Input structure
- The input is a square grid whose non-mask pattern is symmetric under the
  rotations and reflections of the square.
- Color 3 forms one solid rectangular mask over the hidden part of the
  pattern.

Rule
- Return a grid with the same shape as the mask's bounding box.
- For each masked cell, inspect every rotation/reflection-equivalent
  coordinate and ignore any masked cells; the remaining visible colors must
  agree, and that unique color is emitted at the same relative position.

Key insight
- The green rectangle is damage in an otherwise dihedrally symmetric pattern,
  so each hidden pixel can be reconstructed from its visible symmetric twins.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "symmetry/pattern",
  "src": "square dihedral-symmetric color pattern with one solid green rectangular mask",
  "transform": "emit the mask bbox crop, replacing each green pixel by the unique non-green color in its rotation/reflection orbit",
  "grid_shape": "green mask bbox shape"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 24x24 square [observed]
  pattern:
    symmetry: full dihedral D4 (8-fold rotations+reflections of the square)
    colors: 5-8 distinct colors from palette 1-9; color 0 not used [observed]; color 3 reserved for mask
    structure: per-orbit random coloring; 78 orbits total (12 of size 4 on axes/diagonals, 66 of size 8)
  mask:
    color: 3 (green), solid filled rectangle, exactly 1 connected component
    dimensions: 2-8 rows x 3-8 cols, area 9-56 cells (1.6-9.7% of grid)
    placement: anywhere on grid (may touch edges, may cross diagonals/axes)
    recoverability: every masked cell must have >=1 visible (non-masked) D4-symmetric copy
  output: crop of mask bbox; each green cell replaced by the unique color from its visible symmetric copies

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first (build full symmetric pattern, then place mask)
  steps:
    1. Choose n=24. Enumerate all 78 D4 orbits of the n x n grid.
    2. Pick k=5-8 colors from {1,2,4,5,6,7,8,9} (exclude 0 and 3). Assign each orbit a random color from this palette to build the full symmetric pattern.
    3. Choose mask dimensions h in [2,8], w in [2,8] with area in [9,56].
    4. Choose mask top-left (r0,c0) with r0+h<=n, c0+w<=n. Verify every cell in the mask rectangle has >=1 non-masked D4-symmetric copy (reject otherwise; rejection rate is low for small masks on a 24x24 grid).
    5. Overwrite mask cells with color 3 to produce the input grid.
    6. Output is the h x w crop with colors looked up from the full pattern (step 2).
```
</generation_spec>
